const productCases = {"maria":{"name":"MARIA APARTMENTS","industry":"Hospitality / Short-Term Accommodation","platform":"Bilingual website · Booking platform · Administrative dashboard · Telegram automation","location":"Ternopil, Ukraine","role":"Independent Digital Product Designer & Developer","scope":"Business analysis · Product strategy · Brand identity · UX/UI · Development · Integrations · Testing · Deployment","strapline":"From manual apartment rentals to an integrated hospitality management ecosystem","links":[{"label":"MARIA APARTMENTS","href":"https://www.bymariaapartments.com"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>Maria Apartments is a short-term accommodation business operating a portfolio of 19 apartments across several locations in Ternopil, Ukraine.</p><p>The project began with an established rental business that had customers, properties and operational experience, but lacked a unified brand identity and an integrated digital infrastructure.</p><p>Reservations were managed through Excel spreadsheets. Guests relied on direct communication to check availability, confirm bookings and arrange their arrival. Payments involved bank transfers and cash, while key handovers required physical coordination. Instagram served as an important customer-facing channel, but there was no dedicated platform connecting apartment discovery, reservations, payments and internal operations.</p><p>My responsibility was to transform this fragmented experience into a coherent digital ecosystem.</p><p>I independently managed the project across creative direction, user experience, technical implementation and operational integration. The work included establishing a new brand, producing visual content, designing and developing a bilingual accommodation website, implementing a booking and payment system, creating an administrative management platform and developing a custom Telegram bot for guest assistance and contactless check-in.</p><p>The result was not simply a redesigned website.</p><p>It was a connected hospitality management system designed to support the relationship between guests, administrators, reservations, payments, physical properties and daily business operations.</p>"],["Understanding the Business","<h3>The original operating model</h3><p>Before the transformation, the business operated under the generic description &quot;Daily Apartment Rentals&quot; rather than a recognizable independent brand.</p><p>Individual apartments were presented primarily through existing listings and social media. Customers frequently needed to contact an administrator to obtain information that could otherwise have been available through a self-service platform.</p><p>The reservation process depended on several disconnected interactions.</p><p>A potential guest would inquire about an apartment, ask about available dates, receive pricing information and coordinate payment directly with the business.</p><p>A security deposit was typically transferred to a bank card, while the remaining accommodation cost was paid in cash upon arrival.</p><p>After booking, the administrator recorded reservation details in Excel, coordinated arrival arrangements, managed physical keys and later processed the deposit return.</p><p>Other activities, including cleaning schedules, property maintenance and financial tracking, required additional manual coordination.</p><p>These processes were functional individually, but their separation created unnecessary dependencies.</p><h3>The core business problems</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Fragmented information*</em></p><p>Reservation records, apartment availability, payments, guest communication and operational tasks were managed through separate channels.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Administrative dependency*</em></p><p>Routine guest questions and booking actions required direct staff involvement.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited self-service*</em></p><p>Customers could not complete the entire accommodation journey independently through an official website.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Payment friction*</em></p><p>Financial transactions were divided between different payment methods and stages of the stay.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Operational complexity*</em></p><p>Managing 19 properties required ongoing coordination of arrivals, departures, cleaning, maintenance and availability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Inconsistent brand presentation*</em></p><p>The absence of a unified identity made the business appear as a collection of rental listings rather than a recognizable accommodation provider.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited reporting*</em></p><p>Without integrated analytics, evaluating occupancy, reservation activity and property-level performance required manual work.</p><p>The challenge was to address these problems as parts of one system rather than introduce isolated solutions.</p>"],["Discovery & UX Research","<p>My research approach was strongly influenced by direct participation in the business's daily operations.</p><p>Rather than relying exclusively on a design brief, I gained firsthand insight into guest communication, booking coordination, arrival procedures and administrative responsibilities.</p><p>This allowed me to examine the complete service experience, including interactions that happened outside a conventional website.</p><p>I analyzed how guests obtained information, how availability was confirmed, how payments were coordinated and what administrators needed to prepare an apartment for the next arrival.</p><p>I also examined the relationships between reservations, cleaning schedules, security deposits and physical access.</p><p>This revealed an important distinction between the needs of different users.</p><h3>Guest needs</h3><p>Guests needed to:</p><ul><li>Find suitable accommodation without extensive messaging.</li><li>Compare apartments by location, price, capacity and amenities.</li><li>Understand availability and accommodation costs.</li><li>Complete a reservation with clear payment instructions.</li><li>Receive reliable confirmation.</li><li>Access practical arrival and accommodation information.</li><li>Obtain assistance without repeating their booking details.</li></ul><h3>Administrator needs</h3><p>Administrators needed to:</p><ul><li>View reservations across all 19 apartments.</li><li>Identify occupied and available dates.</li><li>Create and modify bookings.</li><li>Coordinate cleaning between departures and arrivals.</li><li>Track deposits and reservation payments.</li><li>Manage apartment information and operational issues.</li><li>Prepare guests for check-in.</li><li>Control physical keybox access.</li><li>Access structured business reporting.</li></ul><h3>Research conclusion</h3><p>The principal challenge was not a lack of individual digital tools.</p><p>It was the absence of a shared system connecting customer-facing experiences with operational data.</p><p>This conclusion shaped the architecture of the entire project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>Research methodology note: The discovery process was based on operational observation, firsthand experience and workflow analysis rather than a formal controlled user study.</em></p>"],["Product Strategy","<p>I defined the transformation around a connected set of digital and physical touchpoints.</p><p>The strategy had three major objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Establish a recognizable hospitality brand*</em></p><p>Create a consistent identity capable of representing all 19 apartments across digital channels and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Build an independent customer booking journey*</em></p><p>Allow guests to discover accommodation, check dates, understand pricing, book and pay without requiring continuous administrator assistance.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Centralize internal operations*</em></p><p>Provide administrators with a dedicated management environment connected to reservations, payment records, apartment availability, cleaning and guest communication.</p><p>A fourth consideration influenced the entire project: the connection between software and physical accommodation.</p><p>A successful online reservation still needed to result in a guest arriving at the correct property, receiving authorized access and finding an apartment prepared for occupancy.</p><p>This made service design and operational reliability just as important as visual interface design.</p>"],["Brand Identity & Creative Direction","<h3>From a generic rental service to Maria Apartments</h3><p>The transformation began with a new brand identity.</p><p>I developed the name <strong>Maria Apartments</strong>, replacing the original generic positioning with an identifiable hospitality brand.</p><p>The new identity needed to communicate comfort, reliability and professional management without feeling excessively corporate or disconnected from the nature of the accommodation service.</p><p>I designed the logo, established the visual direction and created a consistent system of typography, color and supporting visual elements.</p><p>A warm, light visual approach was selected to make the brand feel welcoming while maintaining clarity across functional interfaces.</p><p>The identity was designed to work in several environments: property listings, booking forms, Instagram content, printed materials and physical guest touchpoints.</p><h3>Photography &amp; property presentation</h3><p>Accommodation is a visually driven service.</p><p>Guests rely heavily on photographs to assess the condition, atmosphere and suitability of a property.</p><p>I photographed apartment interiors, selected and retouched images, and prepared visual assets for consistent presentation across the website and social media.</p><p>The objective was not to make different properties appear identical.</p><p>It was to establish a consistent standard of visual quality while preserving the individual characteristics of each apartment.</p><h3>Physical brand materials</h3><p>I also designed branded business cards and keychains, prepared them for production and coordinated ordering.</p><p>These materials extended the new identity beyond screens.</p><p>The keychains were particularly relevant because physical access was an essential part of the accommodation experience.</p><p>Connecting digital presentation with physical objects helped establish continuity throughout the guest journey.</p>"],["Instagram Rebranding & Digital Presence","<p>Instagram was already an important discovery and communication channel for the business.</p><p>However, the existing account lacked the coherence of a unified brand.</p><p>I redesigned the profile presentation, developed a consistent content direction and established a visual system aligned with the new Maria Apartments identity.</p><p>The work included updated profile information, visual content, post design, publication and organized navigation through Instagram Highlights.</p><p>I also connected the social media presence to the official website.</p><p>This reflected a deliberate strategic decision.</p><p>Instagram would remain a channel for discovery, brand communication and customer engagement, while the website would become the primary environment for property exploration, reservations and payments.</p><p>The objective was to reduce dependence on manual social media conversations as the main booking infrastructure.</p>"],["Website UX/UI Design","<h3>A website designed around the complete booking journey</h3><p>The official Maria Apartments website was developed as a responsive, bilingual platform supporting all 19 apartments.</p><p>Its primary purpose was to transform accommodation discovery into a structured and actionable experience.</p><p>The information architecture was organized around the guest's practical decision-making process:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover → Compare → Evaluate → Check Availability → Book → Pay → Receive Confirmation*</em></p><p>Each stage needed to provide enough information for the user to proceed confidently.</p><h3>Interactive building facade</h3><p>One of the distinctive visual features of the website is its interactive building facade.</p><p>Instead of functioning as a purely decorative homepage illustration, the building provides an alternative way to explore available accommodation.</p><p>The interactive windows connect the visual presentation to apartment discovery.</p><p>This approach gave the business a memorable digital identity while making the interface itself part of the experience.</p><p>However, visual originality was not allowed to replace usability.</p><p>The interactive building was supported by more conventional browsing tools, ensuring that customers could still explore properties through familiar catalog and filtering patterns.</p><p>The design decision balanced brand differentiation with practical navigation.</p><h3>Apartment catalog &amp; filtering</h3><p>The website includes a structured catalog of 19 apartments, allowing guests to explore different properties within a consistent interface.</p><p>Filtering supports practical accommodation decisions, including available dates, guest capacity, location and pricing considerations.</p><p>Because properties are distributed across different areas of Ternopil, location-based navigation was an important requirement.</p><p>The catalog was designed to help visitors narrow their options before committing to a detailed property review.</p><h3>Individual apartment pages</h3><p>Each apartment needed enough information to support an informed booking decision.</p><p>I organized property pages around consistent content categories, including:</p><ul><li>Apartment photography.</li><li>Descriptions and accommodation characteristics.</li><li>Guest capacity.</li><li>Relevant amenities.</li><li>Location information.</li><li>Pricing.</li><li>Availability.</li><li>Booking actions.</li></ul><p>Consistent structure was particularly important for a portfolio containing many different properties.</p><p>It allowed visitors to compare options without repeatedly learning a new page layout.</p><h3>Responsive design &amp; localization</h3><p>The website supports Ukrainian and English.</p><p>Both language versions were integrated into the customer-facing experience to make accommodation information accessible to local and international visitors.</p><p>The responsive design accounts for different screen sizes, including mobile browsing, where property discovery, date selection and booking actions require additional attention to hierarchy and touch interaction.</p>"],["Booking Experience & Payment Integration","<h3>Replacing a fragmented reservation process</h3><p>The original booking journey required multiple conversations, manual availability checks and separate payment arrangements.</p><p>I designed a connected booking experience that brought these actions into a structured digital flow.</p><p>The intended customer journey became:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Apartment Selection → Date Selection → Guest Details → Price Review → Online Payment → Booking Confirmation*</em></p><h3>Availability &amp; reservation validation</h3><p>Accommodation availability cannot be treated as a static piece of information.</p><p>A property may become unavailable between the moment a guest views the calendar and the moment they attempt to complete a reservation.</p><p>For this reason, availability is validated through server-side reservation logic.</p><p>The system includes safeguards against conflicting reservations and overlapping booking dates.</p><h3>Pricing logic</h3><p>The booking system was designed to account for accommodation-specific pricing conditions.</p><p>The checkout calculation can incorporate selected dates, length of stay, guest-related charges and security deposit requirements.</p><p>The aim was to present the relevant booking cost within the reservation process instead of relying on a separate conversation to establish the amount.</p><h3>Full online payment</h3><p>I integrated <strong>WayForPay</strong> to support online payment for the accommodation and security deposit.</p><p>Moving from a combination of bank transfers and cash to an integrated checkout experience was one of the project's most significant operational changes.</p><p>However, introducing digital payment also required careful handling of transaction states.</p><p>The implementation included signed payment requests, server-side verification, webhook processing, transaction status handling and reconciliation mechanisms.</p><p>A successful browser redirect alone was not treated as sufficient evidence of payment completion.</p><p>The system needed to verify the payment independently.</p><h3>Handling failure and uncertainty</h3><p>The reservation process also needed to account for situations where a payment was pending, failed, delayed or reported more than once.</p><p>These conditions informed the design of payment states and reservation safeguards.</p><p>The objective was to avoid both customer confusion and inconsistent booking records.</p><p>This work demonstrated that transactional UX involves more than a checkout interface.</p><p>It requires the visible customer journey and underlying system behavior to remain consistent under real-world conditions.</p>"],["Administrative Dashboard","<h3>Replacing Excel with an operational management system</h3><p>A major component of the project was the development of a dedicated administrative dashboard.</p><p>The original Excel-based workflow stored reservation information but did not provide a connected environment for coordinating the broader accommodation operation.</p><p>I designed and developed an interface organized around the tasks administrators actually needed to perform.</p><p>The dashboard consolidated information and actions that had previously been distributed across spreadsheets, messages and manual processes.</p><h3>Reservation management</h3><p>The system supports administrative reservation workflows, including:</p><ul><li>Reviewing existing reservations.</li><li>Creating bookings manually.</li><li>Modifying reservation details.</li><li>Extending stays.</li><li>Transferring reservations between apartments.</li><li>Managing reservation statuses.</li><li>Accessing guest and booking information.</li></ul><p>Manual booking creation was an important requirement because not every reservation originated through the public website.</p><p>The platform needed to accommodate existing business channels without forcing every customer through the same digital entry point.</p><h3>Visual reservation calendar</h3><p>I implemented a visual administrative calendar to provide a clearer view of apartment occupancy and upcoming bookings.</p><p>For a business managing 19 properties, seeing the relationships between apartments, dates and reservation statuses is more useful than examining isolated spreadsheet rows.</p><p>The calendar supports day-to-day planning and helps administrators understand the operational consequences of booking changes.</p><h3>Cleaning coordination</h3><p>Cleaning schedules are directly connected to check-outs and upcoming arrivals.</p><p>The administrative system introduced structured cleaning records and scheduling information based on reservation activity.</p><p>This helped connect apartment availability with the physical preparation required between guest stays.</p><p>Importantly, the software supports planning and coordination; the actual cleaning remains a human operational responsibility.</p><h3>Maintenance &amp; operational tasks</h3><p>The dashboard includes functionality for tracking maintenance issues and other property-related tasks.</p><p>This provides a more organized alternative to relying entirely on informal messages and separate records.</p><h3>Security deposits</h3><p>Deposit tracking was integrated into the administrative environment.</p><p>The system provides records and visibility around deposit-related responsibilities, while physical or financial actions that require administrator involvement remain controlled operational processes.</p><h3>Administrative UX principles</h3><p>The dashboard was designed around information clarity, operational context and task efficiency.</p><p>The goal was not to reproduce Excel inside a website.</p><p>It was to give administrators a purpose-built environment where reservation data could support meaningful actions.</p>"],["Business Analytics & Reporting","<p>Before the transformation, the business had limited centralized tools for examining operational performance.</p><p>Information relevant to occupancy, reservation value and individual properties existed, but evaluating it required manual calculation or reconciliation.</p><p>I introduced an analytics and reporting layer within the administrative platform.</p><p>The system organizes business information around reservation activity, occupancy, financial records and property-level performance.</p><p>This allows administrators to examine historical and upcoming activity through structured reporting rather than disconnected records.</p><p>The design also distinguishes operational information needed for immediate decisions from broader reporting used for planning.</p><p>The project established the infrastructure for better visibility into the business.</p><p>It did not, however, establish verified long-term improvements in revenue or occupancy attributable to the new platform.</p>"],["Telegram Bot & Guest Experience Automation","<h3>Designing the experience beyond checkout</h3><p>Completing a reservation is not the end of an accommodation journey.</p><p>Guests still need practical information about arrival, access, apartment rules and their stay.</p><p>Previously, much of this information was provided through direct communication with an administrator.</p><p>I developed a custom Telegram bot to support the guest experience after booking.</p><p>The bot was built with Python and aiogram and connected to the reservation ecosystem.</p><h3>Personalized booking access</h3><p>An early version of the concept involved reservation-specific MA codes.</p><p>During development, this approach evolved into personalized Telegram links.</p><p>Rather than remembering and manually entering a code, guests could open a reservation-linked Telegram experience.</p><p>The flow includes phone verification and account binding, allowing booking information to be associated with an authorized Telegram account.</p><p>This reduced friction in the intended guest journey while supporting controlled access to reservation details.</p><h3>Guest assistance</h3><p>The bot provides structured access to information relevant to the guest's stay.</p><p>This includes:</p><ul><li>Reservation information.</li><li>Pre-arrival guidance.</li><li>Check-in instructions.</li><li>Apartment-specific information.</li><li>FAQ navigation.</li><li>Help resources.</li><li>Administrator contact options.</li></ul><p>The interaction structure was designed around practical guest questions rather than an unnecessarily complex conversational interface.</p><h3>Contactless check-in</h3><p>A central feature was the connection between verified reservations and physical keybox access.</p><p>The system includes operational configuration for six physical keyboxes associated with the apartment portfolio.</p><p>Authorized guests can receive relevant access instructions according to their reservation and the applicable access conditions.</p><p>This required particular attention to the boundary between public information and restricted data.</p><p>An apartment description can be available to anyone.</p><p>Its physical access code cannot.</p><p>The bot therefore separates general guest assistance from information requiring current authorization.</p>"],["Security & Physical Access Control","<p>Because the platform connects digital reservations to physical accommodation, access security was an important design and engineering consideration.</p><p>Several safeguards were implemented across different stages of the guest journey.</p><h3>Reservation verification</h3><p>Restricted instructions are linked to verified booking information.</p><p>The bot checks relevant authorization conditions instead of relying solely on information recorded during a guest's first interaction.</p><p>This matters when a booking is cancelled, modified, completed or no longer authorized for access.</p><h3>Controlled keybox code activation</h3><p>One of the more important operational decisions concerned the relationship between digital records and physical keyboxes.</p><p>Updating a code in software does not automatically change the code installed on a physical lockbox.</p><p>The administrative workflow therefore separates entering a replacement code from confirming its physical activation.</p><p>A replacement code is not made available to guests until the administrator confirms that the physical keybox has been updated.</p><p>This prevents the system from distributing access information that may not yet work in the real world.</p><h3>Conflicting administrative changes</h3><p>The keybox management system also accounts for potential conflicts between administrative actions.</p><p>Version checks help prevent an outdated administrative view from overwriting more recent code information.</p><h3>Booking and payment integrity</h3><p>The reservation system incorporates server-side safeguards, including payment verification, duplicate-processing protection and database-level measures against overlapping bookings.</p><p>These safeguards are not primarily visual design features.</p><p>They are essential parts of making the customer-facing experience dependable.</p>"],["Technical Architecture & Development","<p>My work extended beyond product strategy and interface design into the implementation and integration of the underlying digital infrastructure.</p><p>The platform was developed as several connected components with distinct responsibilities.</p><h3>Public website</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Next.js · React · TypeScript*</em></p><p>The customer-facing web application supports apartment discovery, responsive interfaces, availability information and booking interactions.</p><h3>Database</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Neon PostgreSQL*</em></p><p>The database supports structured reservation data, apartment information and the relationships required by booking and administrative workflows.</p><h3>Payment system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*WayForPay*</em></p><p>The integration handles checkout requests, payment verification, status updates and transaction processing safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative platform</h3><p>The management interface connects reservation data with operational workflows, including property availability, cleaning schedules, guest records, financial tracking and reporting.</p><h3>Telegram automation</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Python · aiogram*</em></p><p>The separately developed bot provides authenticated guest assistance and controlled access to booking-related information.</p><h3>Hosting &amp; infrastructure</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Vercel · Render · SQLite · GitHub*</em></p><p>The website was deployed through Vercel.</p><p>The Telegram bot was migrated from Railway to Render, with persistent storage used for its operational state.</p><p>GitHub supported source control and the development workflow.</p><h3>My development responsibility</h3><p>I independently directed and implemented the connected system using modern development tools and AI-assisted workflows.</p><p>My responsibilities included translating business requirements into technical structures, designing interfaces, developing application functionality, coordinating integrations, evaluating system behavior, debugging and deployment.</p><p>The project required continuity between design decisions and engineering implementation.</p><p>A booking interaction could affect the database.</p><p>A payment confirmation could affect reservation status.</p><p>A change to a reservation could affect Telegram access permissions.</p><p>Understanding these dependencies was essential to delivering the project as a coherent product.</p>"],["Iteration & Problem-Solving","<p>The project was developed iteratively rather than through a completely fixed sequence of design and development phases.</p><p>Several important decisions changed as operational requirements became clearer.</p><h3>Iteration 01: MA codes to personalized links</h3><p><strong>Initial approach:</strong> Guests would access reservation information using manually entered booking codes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> This introduced an additional step and required guests to retain and enter a code.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Personalized Telegram links connected reservations to a verification and account-binding flow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Reduce unnecessary interaction while preserving controlled access to booking information.</p><h3>Iteration 02: Payment confirmation and reservation reliability</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Payment completion involved multiple systems and potentially asynchronous responses.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Browser redirects and payment notifications could not be treated as perfectly synchronized or independently authoritative.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Server-side verification, reconciliation and duplicate-processing safeguards became part of the transaction workflow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Protect reservation and financial records against delayed or repeated payment events.</p><h3>Iteration 03: Database-level booking protection</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Availability displayed in the interface could change while a customer was completing a reservation.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Interface validation alone could not reliably prevent conflicting bookings.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Additional server-side and database-level safeguards were incorporated.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Maintain reservation integrity even when multiple actions occur close together.</p><h3>Iteration 04: Physical keybox activation</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Digital code updates and physical lockbox changes happened through different processes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> A stored code could be distributed before the physical keybox was updated.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Separate code entry from administrator-confirmed physical activation.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Keep software permissions consistent with the real-world state of the property.</p><h3>Iteration 05: Infrastructure refinement</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> The Telegram service required reliable hosting and persistence of operational data.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> The bot was migrated from Railway to Render with attention to preserving stored information.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Support the continuity of a separately hosted service connected to the wider booking ecosystem.</p><p>These iterations illustrate an important aspect of the project: decisions were evaluated against operational requirements, not only against the appearance of individual interfaces.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The transformation changed how the business presented itself and how its core processes were organized.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Business Area</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>Generic apartment rental positioning</td><td>Maria Apartments brand identity</td></tr><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>Instagram and external listings</td><td>Branded social presence and official website</td></tr><tr><td>Apartment discovery</td><td>Direct inquiries and separate listings</td><td>Structured catalog of 19 apartments</td></tr><tr><td>Availability</td><td>Manual checks and spreadsheets</td><td>Integrated availability calendars</td></tr><tr><td>Booking</td><td>Administrator-coordinated process</td><td>Structured online reservation flow</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Bank transfer and cash</td><td>Integrated WayForPay checkout</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Excel-based management</td><td>Dedicated management dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>Manual scheduling</td><td>Reservation-connected cleaning records</td></tr><tr><td>Guest communication</td><td>Direct messaging and calls</td><td>Personalized Telegram assistance</td></tr><tr><td>Check-in</td><td>Physical coordination and key handover</td><td>Controlled contactless access workflow</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Manual calculations</td><td>Centralized operational analytics</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The value of these changes lies in their connection.</p><p>A redesigned website alone would not have resolved spreadsheet-based administration.</p><p>A booking form alone would not have solved payment verification.</p><p>A Telegram bot alone would not have provided reliable authorization.</p><p>The project required these elements to function as parts of one service.</p>"],["Results & Delivered Outcomes","<p>The project produced a complete set of interconnected creative, technical and operational deliverables.</p><h3>Brand transformation</h3><p>A new name, logo, visual identity, photography direction, social media presentation and printed brand materials established a consistent identity for the accommodation business.</p><h3>Customer-facing platform</h3><p>The business gained an official bilingual website presenting all 19 apartments through structured listings, individual property information, availability and booking functionality.</p><h3>Digital reservations &amp; payment</h3><p>A connected booking experience introduced online checkout, pricing calculations, payment-status handling and server-side reservation safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative management</h3><p>A centralized dashboard introduced structured reservation management, visual calendars, guest records, cleaning coordination, deposit tracking, maintenance records and business reporting.</p><h3>Automated guest assistance</h3><p>The Telegram system provided personalized booking access, verification, structured information and controlled check-in instructions.</p><h3>Connected infrastructure</h3><p>The website, database, payment gateway, administrative system and Telegram bot were integrated into a shared digital operating environment.</p><h3>Validation &amp; measurement</h3><p>The project documentation includes automated tests and deployment checks supporting the technical implementation.</p><p>However, the available evidence does not establish a controlled long-term comparison of revenue, conversion rates, customer satisfaction or operational cost savings.</p><p>Some real-world scenarios, including comprehensive production payment validation and physical check-in success rates, require further documented evaluation.</p><p>For this reason, I distinguish between delivered functionality, expected operational benefits and independently measured business outcomes.</p><p>The most defensible result is the transformation of previously fragmented workflows into a connected digital system.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>Maria Apartments was a multidisciplinary project in which I worked across responsibilities commonly divided between several specialist roles.</p><p>My involvement began with understanding the business and continued through creative direction, interface design, technical development and deployment.</p><p>I was responsible for translating operational problems into product requirements, determining the experience across customer and administrative interfaces, implementing connected functionality and refining decisions as technical and business constraints emerged.</p><p>The project combined several professional disciplines:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Product Strategy*</em></p><p>Understanding the rental operation, defining requirements and organizing the relationship between digital tools and business processes.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI &amp; Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Designing discovery, booking, payment, administration and guest assistance experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Identity &amp; Creative Direction*</em></p><p>Establishing a recognizable identity and applying it consistently across digital and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Photography &amp; Content Production*</em></p><p>Creating and preparing property photography, branded visuals and social media materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Web &amp; Application Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive interfaces, server-side functionality, database connections and administrative features.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Integrations &amp; Automation*</em></p><p>Connecting payment processing, reservation records, Telegram assistance and physical access workflows.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Deployment*</em></p><p>Validating functionality, addressing integration challenges and managing production-oriented technical delivery.</p><p>This breadth of responsibility required an understanding of the entire service rather than a narrow focus on one design deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>Maria Apartments fundamentally expanded the scope of what began as a branding and digital presence project.</p><p>The most important challenge was not creating a visually attractive website.</p><p>It was developing a reliable relationship between customer interactions, administrative processes, financial transactions, stored data and physical accommodation.</p><p>Working directly with the business revealed how easily a small operational detail can affect the wider user experience.</p><p>A confusing payment state can create uncertainty for a guest.</p><p>An overlapping reservation can create a serious administrative problem.</p><p>An incorrectly activated keybox code can prevent someone from entering an apartment.</p><p>A disconnected cleaning schedule can affect whether accommodation is ready for the next arrival.</p><p>These are not isolated interface problems.</p><p>They are service problems that require coordinated product, design and engineering decisions.</p><p>The experience reinforced the importance of understanding a business before selecting technologies or designing screens.</p><p>It also demonstrated the value of maintaining responsibility across the complete development process.</p><p>Maria Apartments represents my ability to work independently across brand identity, product strategy, UX/UI design, software development, technical integrations and operational automation.</p><p>The final result is more than a website.</p><p>It is a connected digital foundation for an accommodation business, built around the practical needs of guests, administrators and the service itself.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*A complete hospitality experience, designed from the first impression to the apartment door.*</em></p>"]]},"arced":{"name":"ARCED","industry":"Construction / Home Improvement","platform":"Two commercial websites · Digital presence","location":"Winnipeg, Canada","role":"UX/UI · Web Design · Development · Brand Identity","scope":"Information architecture · Responsive UX/UI · Service presentation · Implementation","strapline":"A connected digital presence for two related businesses","links":[{"label":"ARCED Construction","href":"https://arcedconstruction.ca"},{"label":"ARCED Tile","href":"https://arcedtile.ca"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","ARCED is one commercial case covering two connected but independent websites for construction and specialist interior finishing services."],["Business Context","The two related businesses needed a coherent family resemblance without becoming duplicate websites. Each site also had to explain its own services and give potential clients a clear route to enquire."],["The Design Challenge","The work required a shared digital language that could flex across construction and tile services while keeping each service category independent and understandable."],["Information Architecture","Separate service architectures organise the content around the questions each audience needs answered. The structure makes it easier to understand the offer, evaluate credibility and move towards contact."],["UX & Visual Direction","The visual direction preserves a relationship between the two brands while adapting the presentation to their different audiences and service contexts."],["Implementation","The scope included responsive UX/UI, visual direction, service presentation and implementation for both published websites."],["Final Product & Deliverables","The result is two connected but independent commercial websites: ARCED Construction and ARCED Tile, each with its own service presentation and enquiry path."],["Reflection & Competencies","ARCED demonstrates information architecture, responsive UX/UI, web design, brand identity and the adaptation of one visual system across related businesses."]]},"vanta":{"name":"Monday Balance","industry":"Financial Technology","platform":"iOS / Android","location":"New York, USA","role":"UX/UI Designer · Product Designer","scope":"Product research · Information architecture · Interaction design · UI design · Prototyping","strapline":"Making personal finance understandable","sections":[["Project Overview","Monday Balance is a personal finance management application designed to help people understand spending habits, organise budgets and make more informed financial decisions. The product focuses on the gap between recording transactions and understanding what those transactions mean.","The work covered product research, UX strategy, information architecture, interaction design, UI design and prototyping."],["Context & Problem Definition","Many finance applications expose users to complex dashboards, excessive categorisation and accounting terminology. Users could see individual transactions but struggled to recognise broader patterns, connect everyday spending with long-term goals or maintain a budgeting system that demanded constant effort."],["Users & Their Needs","The product considered young professionals with regular income, freelancers managing irregular income and goal-oriented savers. Their practical questions were simple: Can I afford this? Where did my money go? What progress am I making? Those questions shaped the hierarchy more than financial terminology did."],["Research & Discovery","Discovery focused on personal financial routines and on the moments when people actually need information. The key design insight was cognitive effort: the product needed to answer an immediate question first, then offer deeper analysis when the user chose to explore it."],["UX Strategy & Information Architecture","The application was organised around five destinations: Overview, Activity, Budgets, Goals and Insights. Overview became the primary interaction point, showing current balance, monthly expenditure, remaining budget and one relevant insight instead of every available metric."],["Key Design Decisions","Progressive disclosure moved detailed analytics into secondary screens. Visual budget tracking replaced dense tables with labelled progress indicators. Flexible income management supported both fixed salaries and irregular income. Quick-add transactions, suggested categories and reusable templates reduced repetitive input friction."],["UI & Interaction Design","Dark surfaces, restrained accents, large type and generous spacing created a calmer alternative to trading-platform interfaces. Important values received clear typographic priority. Empty states, incomplete transactions, warnings and non-colour status cues were considered as part of the core experience."],["Iteration & Design Evolution","The first dashboard placed too many analytical cards in competition for attention. The structure was simplified: secondary metrics moved into expandable views, while the main screen kept information directly related to everyday decisions. The savings experience was also reframed around goal progress rather than transaction history."],["Final Product & Deliverables","The final mobile product brings together daily expense management, budget planning, savings goals and financial analytics. Its design connects short-term actions with longer-term goals without requiring accounting knowledge."],["Reflection & Competencies","The defining task was deciding which information deserved attention, not drawing financial charts. Monday Balance demonstrates mobile UX, financial data visualisation, information architecture, progressive disclosure, accessible UI and product thinking."]]},"vetra":{"name":"Willow Practice","industry":"Veterinary Healthcare / SaaS","platform":"Responsive Web Application","location":"Utrecht, Netherlands","role":"UX/UI Designer · Product Designer","scope":"Workflow analysis · Information architecture · Dashboard design · User flows · Prototyping","strapline":"Designing a more efficient veterinary workflow","sections":[["Project Overview","Willow Practice is a veterinary practice management platform that centralises appointments, animal records, treatment histories and internal clinic operations. It supports small clinics where responsibilities are shared between receptionists, veterinarians and administrators."],["Context & Problem Definition","Small practices often combine paper records, spreadsheets, messaging and disconnected calendars. Patient information becomes difficult to retrieve, follow-up care is tracked manually and owners lack a consolidated view of daily operations."],["Users & Their Needs","Receptionists create appointments, manage calendars, register patients and communicate with owners. Veterinarians review histories, document examinations, record diagnoses and manage treatment plans. Clinic administrators manage schedules, services, reporting and permissions."],["Research & Discovery","The design began by mapping a veterinary appointment from registration and scheduling through arrival, consultation, treatment documentation, payment recording and follow-up planning. The analysis separated fast administrative interactions from the comprehensive medical context needed during consultations."],["Information Architecture","Six modules organise the platform: Dashboard, Calendar, Patients, Clients, Treatments and Administration. Patient records act as the central object connecting appointments, owners, treatment history and follow-up actions."],["Key Design Decisions","A unified patient profile groups identification, owner details, medical history and upcoming appointments chronologically. A split-view calendar keeps appointment context visible. Role-based navigation reduces irrelevant actions, while structured consultation records separate symptoms, findings, diagnosis and recommendations."],["UI & Visual Direction","Neutral surfaces, restrained teal accents, consistent status indicators and clear typography support calm, readable work. Medical information remains visually distinct from administrative information, with attention to warnings, incomplete records, conflicts and tablet use in consultation rooms."],["Iteration & Design Evolution","The first patient profile gave owner information and medical records equal visual priority. It was revised to foreground medical history during consultations while keeping owner details accessible. Appointment creation was simplified by reducing required fields at the first step and allowing details to be completed later."],["Final Product & Deliverables","Willow Practice connects patient records, scheduling, staff workflows and treatment documentation in one system. The result is a more predictable path between related records and fewer disconnected administrative steps."],["Reflection & Competencies","Willow Practice required reliability and information density rather than visual novelty. It demonstrates B2B SaaS design, healthcare UX, complex dashboards, role-based interfaces, workflow architecture and operational product thinking."]]},"moveo":{"name":"Parcel District","industry":"Logistics & Delivery","platform":"Web Dashboard + Mobile Application","location":"Singapore","role":"Product Designer · UX/UI Designer","scope":"Service design · Multi-platform UX · Operational workflows · Dashboard design · Mobile UX","strapline":"Connecting dispatchers, couriers and deliveries","sections":[["Project Overview","Parcel District is a delivery operations platform for a local courier service handling same-day deliveries for small businesses. It combines a desktop dispatch dashboard with a mobile courier application for work under constant time pressure."],["Context & Problem Definition","Dispatchers may coordinate many orders through phone calls and messaging, while couriers need immediate access to the relevant delivery information. The product needed to coordinate people, destinations, statuses and changing conditions without exposing either role to unnecessary complexity."],["Users & Their Needs","Dispatchers need an operational overview, assignment controls, delivery statuses and exception handling. Couriers need a short sequence for viewing the next delivery, confirming progress and reporting a problem while moving between locations."],["Research & Discovery","The work analysed the delivery lifecycle from order creation and assignment through pickup, transit, delivery confirmation and exception resolution. This exposed the need for a shared status model with role-specific actions rather than two disconnected products."],["Information Architecture","The dispatcher workspace centres on active deliveries, assignments, courier availability, locations, exceptions and reporting. The courier experience prioritises assigned tasks, route context, delivery details, status updates and contact actions."],["Key Design Decisions","A shared delivery-status system keeps both interfaces aligned. The dashboard uses compact tables, contextual panels and map-based information for coordination. The courier app uses larger touch targets, simplified navigation and high-contrast primary actions for changing environments."],["UI & Interaction Design","The product separates actionable information from historical reporting. Operational states are recognisable and consistent, while mobile actions remain short and predictable. Exception handling is treated as a first-class flow rather than an error afterthought."],["Iteration & Design Evolution","An early dashboard displayed too many operational metrics at once. It was reorganised around actionable information, with historical reporting moved into a separate area. The courier workflow was shortened so common status updates required fewer interactions."],["Final Product & Deliverables","Parcel District connects assignment management, delivery tracking, operational statuses and exception handling across dispatcher and courier interfaces. The experience reduces unnecessary coordination overhead while preserving awareness of changing conditions."],["Reflection & Competencies","The central design challenge was service coordination, not isolated screens. Parcel District demonstrates logistics UX, service design, map-based UI, multi-platform systems, workflow design and complex information architecture."]]},"modo":{"name":"Atelier Learning","industry":"Education & Creative Industries","platform":"Responsive Web Application","location":"Melbourne, Australia","role":"UX/UI Designer · Digital Product Designer","scope":"UX strategy · Learning experience design · User flows · Interaction design · Prototyping","strapline":"Reimagining the digital learning experience","sections":[["Project Overview","Atelier Learning is an online learning platform for a creative education business offering courses in illustration, visual communication and digital design. It combines educational content, project submissions, instructor feedback and progress tracking."],["Context & Problem Definition","Small creative schools often combine video hosting, file sharing, messaging and manually maintained progress records. Students lose the thread between lessons, tasks and feedback, while teachers need an efficient overview of multiple projects and revisions."],["Users & Their Needs","Beginners need clear structure and confidence that progress is being made. Experienced students need flexibility and access to previous resources. Instructors need submission histories, assignment management and efficient feedback workflows."],["Research & Discovery","The product strategy considered both structured learning and independent exploration. The analysis focused on where students need direction, where they need freedom to revisit material and how an instructor can review practical work without losing its history."],["Information Architecture","Five areas organise the platform: My Learning, Courses, Workspace, Feedback and Profile. Each course connects its lessons to assignments and feedback history, reducing the need to search across unrelated sections."],["Key Design Decisions","A learning dashboard highlights active courses, the next recommended lesson, pending assignments and recent feedback. Assignments receive a dedicated workspace for files and explanations. Feedback is an ongoing revision process, supporting comments, resubmissions and previous versions."],["UI & Visual Direction","Warm neutrals, editorial typography, restrained accents and generous image areas support creative work without turning the interface into a game. Progress indicators communicate milestones rather than competition, while project previews receive more emphasis than administrative metadata."],["Iteration & Design Evolution","The first course interface placed lessons and assignments in one continuous list, making their relationship difficult to understand. The revised structure separated content from tasks while keeping the connection through modules. Feedback history was also redesigned to make revisions easier to locate."],["Final Product & Deliverables","Atelier Learning brings courses, creative assignments, instructor feedback and progress into one coherent learning experience. It supports independent study while preserving the interaction between students and teachers."],["Reflection & Competencies","Atelier Learning treats learning as a continuous process rather than a collection of pages. The project demonstrates EdTech UX, learning experience design, content architecture, student dashboards, interaction design and engagement without unnecessary gamification."]]}};
if (typeof module !== "undefined") module.exports = { productCases };
